From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rocket: retire a pooling job on the PPU's completion

rocket_job_hw_submit() arms INTERRUPT_MASK with the DPU pair and
rocket_job_irq_handler() returns IRQ_NONE for anything else.  That is the
right completion for a convolution or a matmul and there is one class of
program for which it can never arrive: a POOL.

PC_OPERATION_ENABLE is a per-block bitmap.  A convolution enables
CNA/CORE/DPU/DPU_RDMA with 0x1d; a pool enables PPU and PPU_RDMA with
0x60, and the two bit sets are disjoint.  A pooling program has no DPU
stage at all, so DPU_0/DPU_1 never set for it, the only completion it can
raise is masked off, and no interrupt arrives.  drm_sched then retires the
job at JOB_TIMEOUT_MS and takes the core through a reset.

The answer is correct throughout -- the hardware finishes in microseconds
and the output BO holds the right surface -- so nothing but the wall shows
it.  Measured on a Turing RK1 at 600 MHz through both of librocketnpu's
pool entries, C=8 over a 7x7 plane with a 7x7 average window, six calls
each:

  rocket_pool_fp16()           530.6 533.3 506.7 506.6 506.7 506.5 ms
  rocket_global_avgpool_fp16() 506.8 506.6 506.8 533.2 506.8 506.5 ms

twelve submits and twelve "NPU job timed out" lines, the floor being the
500 ms deadline plus the scheduler's tick.  There is no shape or size
dependence because nothing about the program is being waited on.  Whole
gates: the library's pool_fp16 suite is 5.87 s over 11 submits and its
reduce_mean suite 22.0 s over 33, against 0.027 s for the same binary and
the same reductions on a driver that is told which block finishes the job.

The PPU raises its own completion in the same register, two bits over.
Arm that instead when the job's last program is a PPU program.  Which
class a job is in is not visible to the driver and is entirely visible to
userspace, which wrote PC_OPERATION_ENABLE -- so it is a per-job flag.
The mask is latched into the core at submit so the hard IRQ handler still
needs no job pointer and no lock.

Setting the flag on a job whose last program is a convolution costs that
job the same timeout, so it is a hint that has to be right rather than an
optimisation.  A job that MIXES DPU and PPU programs in one chained stream
must not set it, because an interior program's PPU bit would retire the
job while a later DPU write was still draining; the flag is documented as
naming the LAST program's class.

The interface version goes to 1.3 so userspace can tell a kernel that
honors the flag from one that would reject it -- this ioctl rejects an
unknown flag word rather than ignoring it, so "advisory" is a property of
the flag's meaning and not of sending it to an older kernel.  1.2 and bit
1 are left claimed by the RK3576 series' DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE, which
names a completion class for a driver that POLLS for it and has no meaning
here; skipping them keeps one uAPI across both parts, which is what
userspace already gates on.

rk3576/npu 0021 is the same change against that series' poll path, and its
reasoning that "RK3588 takes a completion interrupt and does not poll, so
it is untouched" is what this patch corrects: the interrupt it takes is
DPU-only.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h | 10 +++++++
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c  |  7 ++++-
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c  | 48 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h  |  9 ++++++
 include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h    | 23 ++++++++++++++
 5 files changed, 91 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
index 0abfe2c39..b81df60cf 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
@@ -77,6 +77,16 @@ struct rocket_core {
 	struct mutex job_lock;
 	struct rocket_job *in_flight_job;
 
+	/*
+	 * Which INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS bits are the in-flight job's completion: the
+	 * DPU's for a convolution or a matmul, the PPU's for a pooling program,
+	 * which has no DPU stage at all. Latched by rocket_job_hw_submit() so the
+	 * hard IRQ handler needs no job pointer and no lock -- it runs before the
+	 * thread that would take job_lock, and a job cannot be in flight on this
+	 * core while another is being submitted to it.
+	 */
+	u32 irq_done_mask;
+
 	spinlock_t fence_lock;
 
 	struct {
diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
index 3d978f32a..21edf03da 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
@@ -257,6 +257,11 @@ DEFINE_DRM_ACCEL_FOPS(rocket_accel_driver_fops);
  *         the v1 layout upward, and a trailing field this kernel does not know
  *         is refused with -E2BIG rather than silently ignored.
  * - 1.1 - adds DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED (per-job chained submit).
+ * - 1.2 - RESERVED. DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE names a completion class for a
+ *         driver that POLLS for it; this one takes an interrupt and has no such
+ *         path, so the bit and the version are left claimed rather than reused,
+ *         and one uAPI describes both parts.
+ * - 1.3 - adds DRM_ROCKET_JOB_PPU_DONE (retire on the PPU's completion).
  *
  * Userspace MUST NOT self-chain a job's regcmd layout unless the driver reports
  * >= 1.1: an older kernel does not know the flag, would run that chained layout
@@ -264,7 +269,7 @@ DEFINE_DRM_ACCEL_FOPS(rocket_accel_driver_fops);
  */
 static const struct drm_driver rocket_drm_driver = {
 	.major			= 1,
-	.minor			= 1,
+	.minor			= 3,
 	.driver_features	= DRIVER_COMPUTE_ACCEL | DRIVER_GEM,
 	.open			= rocket_open,
 	.postclose		= rocket_postclose,
diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index 186d5999b..722e0b98e 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -165,6 +165,7 @@ static void rocket_job_hw_submit(struct rocket_core *core, struct rocket_job *jo
 {
 	struct rocket_task *task;
 	unsigned int extra_bit;
+	u32 mask, clear;
 
 	/* Don't queue the job if a reset is in progress */
 	if (atomic_read(&core->reset.pending))
@@ -202,8 +203,31 @@ static void rocket_job_hw_submit(struct rocket_core *core, struct rocket_job *jo
 	rocket_pc_writel(core, REGISTER_AMOUNTS,
 			 PC_REGISTER_AMOUNTS_PC_DATA_AMOUNT((task->regcmd_count + 1) / 2 - 1));
 
-	rocket_pc_writel(core, INTERRUPT_MASK, PC_INTERRUPT_MASK_DPU_0 | PC_INTERRUPT_MASK_DPU_1);
-	rocket_pc_writel(core, INTERRUPT_CLEAR, PC_INTERRUPT_CLEAR_DPU_0 | PC_INTERRUPT_CLEAR_DPU_1);
+	/*
+	 * Arm the block that finishes THIS job. PC_OPERATION_ENABLE is a per-block
+	 * bitmap and the two classes are disjoint: a convolution or a matmul
+	 * enables CNA/CORE/DPU/DPU_RDMA and raises DPU_0/DPU_1, a pool enables PPU
+	 * and PPU_RDMA and raises PPU_0/PPU_1. Arming the DPU pair for a pool masks
+	 * off the only completion it can raise, so the job takes no interrupt at
+	 * all and drm_sched retires it at JOB_TIMEOUT_MS through a reset -- half a
+	 * second per pool submit, with the right answer already in the output BO.
+	 * Which class a job is in is not visible here and is entirely visible to
+	 * userspace, which wrote PC_OPERATION_ENABLE.
+	 */
+	if (job->ppu_done) {
+		mask = PC_INTERRUPT_MASK_PPU_0 | PC_INTERRUPT_MASK_PPU_1;
+		clear = PC_INTERRUPT_CLEAR_PPU_0 | PC_INTERRUPT_CLEAR_PPU_1;
+	} else {
+		mask = PC_INTERRUPT_MASK_DPU_0 | PC_INTERRUPT_MASK_DPU_1;
+		clear = PC_INTERRUPT_CLEAR_DPU_0 | PC_INTERRUPT_CLEAR_DPU_1;
+	}
+	/*
+	 * The mask, clear and raw-status bits share their positions, so the handler
+	 * can test the raw word against the value armed here.
+	 */
+	WRITE_ONCE(core->irq_done_mask, mask);
+	rocket_pc_writel(core, INTERRUPT_MASK, mask);
+	rocket_pc_writel(core, INTERRUPT_CLEAR, clear);
 
 	rocket_pc_writel(core, TASK_CON, PC_TASK_CON_RESERVED_0(1) |
 					 PC_TASK_CON_TASK_COUNT_CLEAR(1) |
@@ -568,8 +592,7 @@ static irqreturn_t rocket_job_irq_handler(int irq, void *data)
 	WARN_ON(raw_status & PC_INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS_DMA_READ_ERROR);
 	WARN_ON(raw_status & PC_INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS_DMA_WRITE_ERROR);
 
-	if (!(raw_status & PC_INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS_DPU_0 ||
-	      raw_status & PC_INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS_DPU_1))
+	if (!(raw_status & READ_ONCE(core->irq_done_mask)))
 		return IRQ_NONE;
 
 	rocket_pc_writel(core, INTERRUPT_MASK, 0x0);
@@ -593,6 +616,14 @@ int rocket_job_init(struct rocket_core *core)
 	spin_lock_init(&core->fence_lock);
 	mutex_init(&core->job_lock);
 
+	/*
+	 * Until a job arms it, accept the class every program before this patch
+	 * raised -- so a stray interrupt on a core that has run nothing behaves
+	 * exactly as it did.
+	 */
+	core->irq_done_mask = PC_INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS_DPU_0 |
+			      PC_INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS_DPU_1;
+
 	core->irq = platform_get_irq(to_platform_device(core->dev), 0);
 	if (core->irq < 0)
 		return core->irq;
@@ -687,7 +718,7 @@ static int rocket_ioctl_submit_job(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_file *file
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	/* Reject unknown flag bits so a future flag cannot be silently ignored. */
-	if (job->flags & ~DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED) {
+	if (job->flags & ~(DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED | DRM_ROCKET_JOB_PPU_DONE)) {
 		drm_dbg(dev, "Unknown bits set in drm_rocket_job.flags (0x%x).\n",
 			job->flags);
 		return -EINVAL;
@@ -714,6 +745,13 @@ static int rocket_ioctl_submit_job(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_file *file
 	 */
 	rjob->want_batched = !!(job->flags & DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED);
 
+	/*
+	 * And which block's completion this job's last program raises. A pool is
+	 * PPU and PPU_RDMA writes with no DPU stage, so the DPU bits never set for
+	 * it and the default arming masks off the only completion it has.
+	 */
+	rjob->ppu_done = !!(job->flags & DRM_ROCKET_JOB_PPU_DONE);
+
 	ret = drm_sched_job_init(&rjob->base,
 				 &file_priv->sched_entity,
 				 1, NULL, file->client_id);
diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h
index 43bee21ba..50ca38601 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h
@@ -44,6 +44,15 @@ struct rocket_job {
 	bool want_batched;
 	bool batched;
 
+	/*
+	 * ppu_done: userspace set DRM_ROCKET_JOB_PPU_DONE -- this job's last
+	 * program is a PPU program (pooling), whose completion is the PPU's own
+	 * bit and not the DPU's. Selects which INTERRUPT_MASK bits are armed and
+	 * which RAW_STATUS bits the handler accepts. Captured at submit from
+	 * drm_rocket_job.flags.
+	 */
+	bool ppu_done;
+
 	/* Fence to be signaled by drm-sched once its done with the job */
 	struct dma_fence *inference_done_fence;
 
diff --git a/include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h b/include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h
index e75db56cc..2cea95faf 100644
--- a/include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h
+++ b/include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h
@@ -37,6 +37,29 @@ extern "C" {
  */
 #define DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED		(1 << 0)
 
+/*
+ * DRM_ROCKET_JOB_PPU_DONE: this job's last program is a PPU program (pooling),
+ * whose completion is the PPU's own bit in INTERRUPT_RAW_STATUS and not the
+ * DPU's.  A PPU program enables no DPU stage at all -- PC_OPERATION_ENABLE is
+ * a per-block bitmap and a pool sets 0x60 against a convolution's 0x1d -- so
+ * without this the driver arms DPU_0/DPU_1, the only completion the job can
+ * raise is masked off, no interrupt arrives, and drm_sched retires the job at
+ * its timeout through a core reset.  Measured at ~507ms per pool submit.
+ *
+ * Userspace knows the class because it wrote PC_OPERATION_ENABLE.  Setting it
+ * on a job whose last program is a convolution costs that job the same timeout,
+ * so it is a hint that has to be right rather than an optimisation.
+ *
+ * A job that ends in a PPU program but also contains DPU programs earlier in a
+ * chained stream must NOT set this: the PPU bit an interior program raised
+ * would retire the job while a later DPU write was still draining.
+ *
+ * Only honored by a driver advertising interface version >= 1.3 (bit 1 and
+ * version 1.2 are claimed by the RK3576 series' DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE,
+ * which has no meaning for a driver that takes an interrupt).
+ */
+#define DRM_ROCKET_JOB_PPU_DONE		(1 << 2)
+
 /**
  * struct drm_rocket_create_bo - ioctl argument for creating Rocket BOs.
  *
-- 
2.43.0
